by dunglas at 2023-10-06T10:05:58Z

# Components Responsibilities

At its core, this new component is a fast and modern JSON parser and encoder. Its responsibility is similar to the responsibility of the existing `Yaml` component but for JSON. It fixes many issues of the native `json_encode` and `json_decode` PHP functions: streaming, on-demand parsing, ability to create strongly typed objects instead of raw associative arrays **in one pass**...

It's why I suggest naming it `Json`, which is consistent with `Yaml`. Both components should also implement the same interfaces, that should be part of Symfony Contracts. In the future, we (or the community) could create new components supporting other formats that implement the same interfaces (TOML, JSON5...).

On the other hand, the `Serializer` component is a very flexible library designed to normalize PHP data structures in raw associative arrays, and then encode them in a large variety of open formats (two passes: normalization then encoding).

Even if there may be some little overlap, the responsibility of both components is very different. Actually, the `Serializer` component could (and should) delegate encoding to the new `Json` component, as it already does to the `Yaml` component for YAML encoding. And for many simple use cases, using the `Json` (or the `Yaml`) component should be enough.

In my opinion, there are only drawbacks to creating a one-size-fits-all component for these different use cases:
* The `Serializer` component is one of the most popular Symfony components, and forcing the community to migrate to a new design will hurt it for no clear benefits
* the current design of the Serializer (2 passes) has been proved useful and very flexible
* keeping `Json` low-level will allow it to focus on what it should do: fast and convenient JSON encoding, without having to introduce too many compromises to make it too generic

In the end, we'll have a set of components, each with a clear responsibility:
* `Json`, `Yaml`, and the like: low-level parsers and encoders for standard and open formats
* `Serializer`: high-level, flexible component to transform PHP data structures to standard formats without requiring a 1-to-1 mapping (normalization); can delegate to `Json`, `Yaml`, etc
* `Mapper` (#51741): copy data from a PHP object of one type to another PHP object of another type

# `Json` Component Design

Just yesterday, @dsnet and the @tailscale team published a design proposal for the future major version of Go's JSON encoder: https://github.com/golang/go/discussions/63397.

Their proposal is of high quality, and what's reassuring is that the design they propose is very close to what we sketched out during the workshops with @mtarld and @soyuka that led to this PR.

Parts of their proposal are better than the current implementation and should be followed:

* Strict compliance with recent JSON-related RFCs ([RFC 4627](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4627), [RFC 7159](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7159), [RFC 7493](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7493), and [RFC 8259](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8259)): in my opinion, compliance with these RFCs is a pre-requisite before the merge
* Split between a low-level parser/encoder (`encoding/json/jsontext`) and a higher-level (`encoding/json`), more complex, one that deals with high-level language data structures (marshaller): this PR already contains the code for that, but the separation between the low-level and higher-level code should be more distinctly marked (two separate sub-namespaces for instance)
* Separate methods (and in our case separate interfaces) to deal with streaming and non-streaming: currently we have a single method for both, but the API would be better by being separated

Here is a quick proposal for the public API for the contracts taking that into account:

```php
namespace Symfony\Contracts\Encoding;

interface MarshalerInterface
{
    public Marshal(mixed $in, array $options = []): string;
}

interface UnmarshalerInterface
{
    public Unmarshal(string $in, array $options = []): mixed;
}

interface StreamingEncoderInterface
{
    public Marshal(mixed $in, array $options = []): StreamWriterInterface; // StreamWriterInterface will wrap a PHP resource, but hide it behind an interface because resources are slowly being deprecated in PHP
}

interface StreamingUnmarshalerInterface
{
    public Unmarshal(StreamReaderInterface $in, array $options = []): mixed;
}
```

The non-streaming marshaller/unmarshaller will be delegated to the streaming one.
The `Json` component should also expose a lower-level, JSON-specific API similar to Go's `jsontext`.

