by dsnet at 2023-10-06T17:14:25Z

Sorry for the drive-by comments, but I was tagged on this :)

> Strict compliance with recent JSON-related RFCs

For v2 JSON in Go, we're rejecting invalid UTF-8 and duplicate object names by default. Essentially, we're planning on targeting [RFC 7493](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7493.html) by default, which is a standard that's fully compliant with [RFC 8259](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259.html) that makes strict decisions about undefined behavior.

For UTF-8 validation, this extends beyond just validating that the JSON text is valid UTF-8, but also that any escape sequences containing surrogate halves resolve to valid Unicode codepoints. The latter is something that many parsers fail to get correct.

We're rejecting duplicate names since they can be lead to security flaws ([CVE-2017-12635](https://docs.couchdb.org/en/stable/cve/2017-12635.html)). Technically, RFC 8259 specifies that duplicate names are undefined behavior, so a compliant parser is free to take the first result, the last result, merge all the results, or just report an error. The main detriment of rejecting duplicate names is that it requires O(n) memory (where n is the number of members in a JSON object). Since Go is a strongly-typed language, we assume that JSON object is most often unmarshaled into a Go struct, where we statically know how many fields exist. In that situation, we just use a bit-map of field indexes to track which JSON object names were already seen. Thus, the performance cost is negligible in most cases.

Tim Bray, the author of RFC 8259, even [recommends on his blog](https://www.tbray.org/ongoing/When/201x/2017/12/14/RFC-8259-STD-90) that implementations should target RFC 7493:
> If you want to use an RFC as foundation for a REST API or some other Internet protocol, I actually don’t recommend 8259, I recommend I-JSON, [RFC 7493](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7493), which describes exactly the same syntax as all the other specs (by referencing 7159), but explicitly rules out some legal-but-dumbass things you could do that might break your protocol, for example using anything but UTF-8 or having duplicate member names in your objects.

> AFAICT Golang does not suffer from this issue: the docs just use both terms interchangeably

Yea, in our documentation, we often use "marshal" and "serialize" interchangeably, but we avoid the term "serialize" throughout the JSON API itself to avoid confusion.

In general, we use:
* the terms "encode" and "decode" to describe syntactic functionality, which is functionality that only deals with JSON at the grammar
* the terms "marshal" and "unmarshal" to describe semantic functionality, which is functionality that assigns meaning to JSON data as Go data and vice-versa

Thus, only "marshal", "unmarshal", "encode", and "decode" in our declared functionality.

